// Tessera stdlib: F128 gamma and ln_gamma, correctly rounded
//
// CORE-MATH has no binary128 gamma, so these run in two tiers, as sin_pi does (F128Extra.tess):
//
// - fast: ln |Γ(x)| in F128Extended (192 bits). Near 1 and 2 (|x - 1| or |x - 2| < 2^-5) by the
//   Taylor series of ln Γ there; else the recurrence lifts x to [30, 31) and Stirling's series
//   takes over; below -1, reflection with an exactly reduced sin πx. Γ is e^(ln |Γ|) with its sign.
//   The result is kept when all of it × (1 ± 2^-band) rounds to one F128.
// - exact: otherwise the same formulas in F128Big (448 bits, Standard/F128Big.tess), lifting to
//   [100, 101), rounded once.
//
// The error of ln |Γ| is measured against its scale, the largest term summed into it (LnGammaWide),
// since near the zeros of ln Γ the sum cancels: ln_gamma's band is F128_LN_GAMMA_BAND less the bits
// the value lies below its scale, and gamma's (whose relative error is ln |Γ|'s absolute one)
// F128_GAMMA_BAND less the scale's exponent. Both bands are measured, not proven: on 43680 inputs
// (random ones near 0 down to the subnormals, around 1 and 2, in (0, 20), up to Γ's overflow at
// 1755.548 and on to 2^16383, negative non-integers of every size, near the negative integers, and
// the F128s around each negative zero of ln Γ), the fast tier's error was at most 2^-189.09 of the
// scale for both ln |Γ| and Γ, so 184 leaves about 5 bits. Only inputs near the negative zeros (and
// inputs chosen near a rounding boundary) reach the exact tier.
//
// The exact tier's error was at most 2^-429.9 max(1, |ln Γ|) on 1167 such inputs. The closest an
// F128 comes to a zero of ln Γ gives |ln Γ| of about 2^-114 near 1 and 2 and 2^-111.5 near the
// negative zeros (at x = -2.457…; from -31 on the zeros lie within one ulp of the integers), so
// even there the result is within 2^-319 relative (measured at x = 1 + 2^-112): far closer than any
// F128 result is expected to lie to a rounding boundary. Some do lie close: Γ(2^-k (1 - 2^-113)) =
// 2^k (1 + 2^-113) + 2^(k-226) - γ + …, within about 2^-226 of a midpoint, and the fast tier sends
// those on (tests/f128_gamma.tess has them).
//
// Exact cases, handled first: Γ(n) = (n - 1)! for the integers 1 <= n <= 40, computed exactly and
// rounded once. Up to n = 38 the odd part of (n - 1)! has at most 113 bits, so Γ(n) is an F128; 38!
// has a 114-bit odd part, so Γ(39) is a midpoint and rounds to even; from 39! on the odd part is
// longer, so no larger factorial is exact or a midpoint. ln Γ is +0 at 1 and 2; at any other
// integer n it is ln (n - 1)!, the logarithm of an integer other than 1, which is transcendental
// (Lindemann), so not an F128 or a midpoint. At the other inputs no argument rules out an exact or
// midpoint result (whether Γ at a non-integer dyadic rational can be one is open); none is known,
// and the exact tier would round it correctly unless it lay within 2^-319 of a boundary without
// being on one.

module Standard::Core

/// The fast tier's bands, in bits below the scale of ln |Γ| (see the note at the top).
preset F128_LN_GAMMA_BAND: S64 = 184
preset F128_GAMMA_BAND:    S64 = 184
/// Below this band the fast tier doesn't try.
preset GAMMA_MIN_BAND: S64 = 120
/// The fast tier lifts x to [30, 31) before Stirling's series, the exact tier to [100, 101).
preset GAMMA_WIDE_LIFT: S64 = 30
preset GAMMA_BIG_LIFT:  S64 = 100

// ln Γ(1 + z) = Σ a_k z^k and ln Γ(2 + z) = Σ b_k z^k, k from 1, to 192 bits: a_1 = -γ, a_k =
// (-1)^k ζ(k) / k; b_1 = 1 - γ, b_k = (-1)^k (ζ(k) - 1) / k (computed with mpmath)
